      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Essex Branch of the Eastern Railroad, built 1870, originally hauled ice from Chebacco Lake and later carried Boston picnickers to Centennial Grove.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.essexwalkingtour.org/historic-tour",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Burnham's Corner in South Essex once housed several small shops and a large shoe factory at the end of the 19th century.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.essexwalkingtour.org/historic-tour",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Old Burying Ground, laid out in 1680, was the site of notorious grave robberies in 1818.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.essexwalkingtour.org/historic-tour",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Essex is known as “America’s Antiques Capital,” home to a long‑standing antique shop scene.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.essexwalkingtour.org/historic-tour",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The Great Marsh and Estuary, the largest contiguous salt marsh north of Long Island, stretches from Cape Ann to Salisbury and borders Essex.",
